## Summary

`layer.new.frameFromLayers` (Layer › New › Frame from Layers) adds two history entries, `Group Layers` and `Frame from Layers`. One `edit.undo` therefore does not return to the document before the command: it leaves the selected layers inside a new group. A user or agent who undoes the command once is left with a document neither before nor after it.

## Runtime or environment

- Release build: `photocraft-cli 0.2.0 (ad8632173, 2026-10-05)` from the v0.2.0 release asset `photocraft-cli-0.2.0-macos-universal.zip`, on macOS 27 arm64.
- Source build: `main` at 93bd44899baf17f28f48ca47a652fb2dd5db2821, built from source (reports `photocraft-cli 0.2.0 (dev build)`). Line numbers below refer to this commit. A source build of 55054b5 gave the same results.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Create any RGB PNG, for example `ffmpeg -f lavfi -i testsrc2=size=192x128 -frames:v 1 rgb8.png`.
2. Start `photocraft-cli serve --automation-read-root <folder containing rgb8.png>` and send these JSON lines one at a time, reading `layers` and `history` from each `doc.inspect` reply:

```json
{"id":1,"method":"doc.open","params":{"path":"rgb8.png"}}
{"id":2,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"layer.new.frameFromLayers"}}
{"id":3,"method":"doc.inspect"}
{"id":4,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"edit.undo"}}
{"id":5,"method":"doc.inspect"}
{"id":6,"method":"engine.execute","params":{"command":"edit.undo"}}
