## Summary

`file.new` and the core filters accept parameter values of the wrong type, misspelled parameter names, unlisted enum values and numbers outside the documented range, and run with a default or clamped value instead, reporting success. This asks for those commands to fail with an error that names the parameter, as a first slice of parameter validation that other commands can follow.

## Problem to solve

Automation clients (MCP `command_run` / `command_batch` / `doc_new`, `photocraft-cli serve` `engine.execute` / `doc.new` / `batch`, and `photocraft-cli run`) build params from the registry hints that `command_list` / `engine.commands` publish, such as `{"radius":0.1..1000=1}` for `filter.blur.gaussianBlur` and `"mode":"rgb|gray|cmyk|lab"="rgb","depth":8|16|32=8` for `file.new`; the MCP `doc_new` schema also lists `mode` as `rgb | gray | cmyk | lab` and `depth` as `8 | 16 | 32`. When a client gets a value or a key wrong, the command still succeeds with something else, so a typo or unit mistake produces a wrong document with no error. Observed on the current `main` source build at 93bd44899baf17f28f48ca47a652fb2dd5db2821 and on the v0.2.0 release CLI (`photocraft-cli 0.2.0 (ad8632173, 2026-10-05)`), on macOS 27.0 arm64, with headless MCP (`photocraft-cli mcp`) and an open 640×480 document:

```text
command_run {"id":"filter.blur.gaussianBlur","params":{"radius":"big"}} -> ok {"filter":{"filter":"gaussianBlur","radius":1.0},"layer":2}
command_run {"id":"filter.blur.gaussianBlur","params":{"radus":3}}      -> ok {"filter":{"filter":"gaussianBlur","radius":1.0},"layer":2}
command_run {"id":"filter.blur.gaussianBlur","params":{"radius":5000}}  -> ok {"filter":{"filter":"gaussianBlur","radius":1000.0},"layer":2}
command_run {"id":"filter.blur.gaussianBlur","params":{"radius":-4}}    -> ok {"filter":{"filter":"gaussianBlur","radius":0.10000000149011612},"layer":2}
doc_new {"width":32,"height":32,"mode":"hsv"}   -> ok; doc_inspect shows composite "RGB", depth 8
doc…

**Version:** 0.2.0 release (ad86321), macOS arm64. Same on main @ 2628b0d.

In Photoshop, right-clicking a Smart Object in the Layers panel offers Edit Contents. In PhotoCraft the context menu of a Smart Object has Convert to Smart Object and Rasterize Layer, but no Edit Contents, so the only way in is Layer › Smart Objects › Edit Contents. The thumbnail double-click on main already helps a lot, thank you. The menu entry would make the right-click path match Photoshop too.

Suggested change in `crates/ui-egui/src/layer_menu_ui.rs`, `entries()`:

```rust
LayerContent::Smart(_) => {
    v.push(Some(("Edit Contents", "layer.smartObjects.editContents")));
    v.push(Some(("Rasterize Layer", "layer.rasterize.smartObject")));
}
```

I run this locally on 0.2.0. The entry goes through `menus::invoke` like the other items and opens the contents document as expected.

## Summary

`photocraft_geom::Rect::width()` and `Rect::height()` compute `x1 - x0` with an unchecked `i32` subtraction, so a rect whose edges span more than `i32::MAX` panics with `attempt to subtract with overflow` in a debug build (and wraps to a value clamped to 0 in a release build). `Rect::translate` and `Rect::inflate` were already made saturating for exactly this class of input, but `width`/`height` were not. The two warp commands build a `Rect` directly from a JSON `rect` parameter by saturating each component from an `f64`, then only reject empty rects, so an extreme but accepted parameter reaches `Rect::width()` inside the resampler and the command fails with the engine's generic internal-error message instead of a parameter error, against the AGENTS.md "Never crash" contract and rule 9.

## Runtime or environment

- Baseline: PhotoCraft `main` at commit 2628b0dd3d667e481e1c0c5e3bf487708b4b1bf5, the default `cargo test` (debug, overflow checks on) profile on macOS.
- The release profile was not exercised; the wrapping arithmetic described in Additional context is static reasoning about `i32` subtraction, not an executed observation.

## Steps to reproduce

Create `crates/engine/tests/zz_hunt_a04_rect_overflow.rs` with the fixture below and run `cargo test -p photocraft-engine --test zz_hunt_a04_rect_overflow -- --nocapture` against the baseline.

