## Summary

An airbrush stroke (`paint.stroke` with a build-up brush, or a built-in Airbrush preset) derives its dab count from the per-point `timeMs` field with no upper bound, so a stroke whose points carry a large timestamp gap emits one dab per build-up interval of that gap. With the build-up rate at its maximum (1000 dabs/s, settable through the brush params) the dab count equals the gap in milliseconds; the built-in Airbrush presets run at 25-30 dabs/s, so the count is the gap divided by about 40. A large gap therefore produces hundreds of millions to billions of dabs: the command runs for minutes or longer and the stroke buffer grows until the allocation fails, which hangs or aborts the app and can lose unsaved work. The command layer already rejects absurd point coordinates for exactly this reason, but the time-driven path has no equivalent guard.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Check out the base revision below and use the project cargo environment.
2. Run the engine integration test below: it paints one `paint.stroke` with two points 4000 ms apart and then one 40000 ms apart, using the build-up brush.
3. Observe that the run time grows with the time gap; a real gap of an hour is 3.6e6 ms and a monotonic-clock glitch or an automation caller can supply far more.

