## Summary

`exif_orientation` only recognises the classic TIFF header (version 42, 12-byte entries, 4-byte offsets) when it looks for the IFD0 Orientation tag, so a BigTIFF (version 43, 20-byte entries, 8-byte offsets) that `detect` reports as `Format::Tiff` is decoded and developed with its Orientation tag ignored. A BigTIFF whose IFD0 says Orientation 6 or 8 opens sideways, while the same pixels in a classic TIFF open upright, contradicting the crate's documented behaviour that a TIFF's IFD0 Orientation tag is applied on decode.

## Steps to reproduce

Build the same 2×1, 8-bit gray image twice, once as a classic TIFF and once as a BigTIFF, both with Orientation 6 (the viewer turns the stored landscape 90° clockwise, so the upright result is 1×2). Save the test below as `crates/codecs/tests/zz_bigtiff_orientation.rs` and run `cargo test -p photocraft-codecs --test zz_bigtiff_orientation`:

```rust
use photocraft_codecs::*;

/// A classic little-endian TIFF, one IFD, 8-bit gray, Orientation in IFD0.
fn classic_tiff(w: u32, h: u32, orientation: u16, pixels: &[u8]) -> Vec<u8> {
    let entries: Vec<(u16, u16, u32, u32)> = vec![
        (256, 3, 1, w), (257, 3, 1, h), (258, 3, 1, 8), (259, 3, 1, 1),
        (262, 3, 1, 1), (274, 3, 1, u32::from(orientation)),
        (273, 4, 1, 0), (277, 3, 1, 1), (278, 3, 1, h), (279, 4, 1, pixels.len() as u32),
    ];
    let ifd_len = 2 + 12 * entries.len() as u32 + 4;
    let strip = 8 + ifd_len;
    let mut b = b"II*\0".to_vec();
    b.extend_from_slice(&8u32.to_le_bytes());
    b.extend_from_slice(&(entries.len() as u16).to_le_bytes());
    for (tag, ty, count, val) in entries {
        let val = if tag == 273 { strip } else { val };
        b.extend_from_slice(&tag.to_le_bytes());
        b.extend_from_slice(&ty.to_le_bytes());
        b.extend_from_slice(&count.to_le_bytes());
        b.extend_from_slice(&val.to_le_bytes());
    }
    b.extend_from_slice(&0u32.to_le_bytes());
    b.extend_from_slice(pixels);
    b
}

