### Summary
After `layer.new.layerViaCopy`, the selection stays active (`hasSelection: true`). Photoshop drops the selection after Layer via Copy (⌘J). Because it stays, the next `edit.transform` works on the selected part only. With a feathered selection the feather is effectively applied twice: the partially selected edge pixels are only partly moved, and a faint copy stays at the source position.

Found while driving PhotoCraft headless over MCP. A typical agent sequence is "select → layerViaCopy → transform", and it leaves visible ghost edges when the layer is later composited over different content.

### Steps (MCP / control channel, headless)
```jsonc
doc_new {"width":200,"height":100,"background":"#000000"}
select.rect {"x":10,"y":10,"width":40,"height":40}
paint.gradient {"from":[0,0],"to":[1,0],"colors":["#ff0000","#ff0000"]}   // red square on Background
select.rect {"x":10,"y":10,"width":40,"height":40,"feather":4}
layer.new.layerViaCopy {}
// doc_inspect here → "hasSelection": true   (Photoshop: no selection)
edit.transform {"matrix":[1,0,0,1,100,0]}
select.deselect {}
layer.setProps {"layer":<Background id>,"visible":false}
document.pixel {"x":11,"y":30}    // → [1,0,0,0.1725]   residue at the source position
document.pixel {"x":111,"y":30}   // → [1,0,0,0.6039]   moved copy, edge alpha reduced
```

### Expected
As in Photoshop: Layer via Copy creates the layer and deselects. Free Transform then transforms the whole new layer. Nothing is left at the source position, and the feathered edge keeps the alpha it had after the copy.

### Actual
The selection persists. The transform applies to the selection, so a feathered copy is split into a moved part and a residue (about 17% alpha at x=11 in the example).

### Notes
- With a hard-edged selection the pixels move correctly. The problem is only visible with feather or anti-aliasing.
- Workaround: `select.deselect` right after `layer.new.layerViaCopy`.

