PhotoCraft needs a **Frequency Separation** feature: two-layer technique (Low Frequency = blurred clone, High Frequency = original − low, on Linear Light). Lets the retoucher smooth skin tone without destroying pores (large soft brush on Low) and remove blemishes without smudging texture (small clone/heal on High). Today in PhotoCraft, the only way to set this up is a 4–5 step manual dance: duplicate the layer, Gaussian-blur the bottom copy, Add Image (Subtract) to the top copy with Scale 2 / Offset 128, set the top layer to Linear Light. That setup is a non-trivial friction point for portrait retouchers evaluating PhotoCraft.

This PR proposal makes Frequency Separation a one-click command

frequency_separator.rs:

`//! Frequency Separation: classical two-layer portrait retouching technique.
//!
//! Builds a "Low Frequency" layer (Gaussian-blurred copy of the source) and a
//! "High Frequency" layer (the difference image, rescaled to mid-grey and
//! 50% amplitude). With the High layer on **Linear Light** over the Low
//! layer, the result equals the source within the depth's quantisation
//! tolerance — so retouchers can smooth tone on the Low layer without
//! losing texture, and remove blemishes on the High layer without smudging
//! colour.
//!
//! ## Math
//!
//! Given a source pixel `S` and the corresponding low-frequency pixel `L`
//! (a Gaussian-blurred copy of `S`):
//!
//! ```text
//! High = (S − L) / 2 + ½
//! ```
//!
//! The "÷ 2" is the `/ Scale` of Photoshop's Apply Image Subtract with
//! `Scale = 2`; the "+ ½" is the `Offset 128` divided by 255.
//!
//! With the High layer on Linear Light over the Low layer:
//!
//! ```text
//! LinearLight(L, High) = clamp(L + 2·High − 1, 0, 1)
//!                     = clamp(L + (S − L) + 1 − 1, 0, 1)
//!                     = clamp(S, 0, 1)
//!                     = S
//! ```
//!
//! So the two layers reconstruct the source exactly (within the working
//! buffer's precision), and editing either layer changes tone …

## What

A focus stack knows the depth of every pixel: the layer it is sharpest in. Edit › Auto-Blend Layers › Stack Images throws that away. Filter › Blur › Lens Blur already takes a depth map (`depthMap: layerMask`) to refocus a picture with a real lens model, so a stack could produce an all-in-focus image *and* the depth map to defocus it again, selectively, afterwards — the classic use of a focus bracket beyond the merge.

## Proposal

A `depthMap` option on `edit.autoBlendLayers` (stack only) that adds a hidden gray **Depth** layer, black = the bottom layer, white = the top, from depth from focus over the aligned layers (`photocraft_algo::dff`), the modern non-learned focus-volume recipe:

1. a per-layer focus measure on luminance, the ring difference filter of Jeon, Surh, Im & Kweon (IEEE TIP 2019), block-averaged to a half-resolution working grid;
2. cost aggregation of every slice with the guided filter (He, Sun & Tang, PAMI 2013), the merged all-in-focus luminance as guide;
3. a streaming per-pixel peak search over the layer axis with the Gaussian interpolation of Nayar & Nakagawa (PAMI 1994) for sub-layer depth, plus a confidence from the peak's prominence, its ratio to the runner-up and a noise gate;
4. edge-aware weighted-least-squares regularisation (Farbman et al., SIGGRAPH 2008) with the confidence as data term, solved by multigrid-preconditioned conjugate gradients from the fast global smoother's separable sweeps (Min et al., IEEE TIP 2014), with one Huber reweighting;
5. guided upsampling to full resolution.

The layer is hidden so the composite stays the merge; copying it onto a layer's mask drives Lens Blur. The result JSON gains `"depth": <layer id>`.

Ported from [lapstack](https://github.com/ragtux/LAPSTACK) (MIT, mine) onto PhotoCraft's buffers and `par_rows`, without lapstack's raw-pointer column iterator (the column sweeps run on transposed planes), no panics, deterministic.

