## What happens
**Layer › Rasterize › Layer** does nothing useful for a **raster layer with layer effects (Drop Shadow, Glow, …) or a vector mask**. The command only converts type, shape, fill and smart object layers; on anything else it answers "the layer has nothing to rasterize". On current `main` the menu item is enabled for such layers (`enable_rules::kind_blocks` lets raster layers with effects or a vector mask through) and then fails; once the availability bug in the Layers menu (#2244) is fixed it is greyed instead (so it no longer lies, but the capability is still missing).

Adobe's help describes Rasterize › Layer as rasterizing "all vector and generated data" on the selected layers (read through search excerpts; the page itself returned 403, and I did not run Photoshop). A vector mask already has **Rasterize › Vector Mask** here; there is no command that bakes layer effects into the pixels.

## Why it is not just "render the layer into a surface"
The only existing way to bake effects, **Convert to Smart Object, then Rasterize**, changes the picture. Measured on a 120×90 document with a white Background and one raster layer with a Drop Shadow (distance 12, size 6) and an Outer Glow (size 8), everything else default: after the round trip **1324 of 10800 pixels (12%)** differ from the composite before. The default effect blend modes (Multiply for the shadow, Screen for the glow) are evaluated against the smart object's transparent backdrop instead of the document, so a Screen glow over white is invisible before and visible after. Any naive bake has the same defect.

So the feature needs a decision first:
- which backdrop the effects are evaluated against, and whether their blend modes are kept or baked;
- the region: effects extend past the layer's pixels and past the canvas (a raster layer may also hold pixels outside the canvas);
- fill opacity, advanced blending (knockout) and the layer mask, which must keep working after the bake;
